Carry commands onto plays that are still loading

A play's file loads outside the command loop, and nothing tied the
commands that arrived meanwhile to the sound being loaded. A speed sent
right after its play ran first, matched nothing and was lost; a
voice_stop sent right after a looping play left the loop running. The
customer's report (play then speed for Crank2, "Command failed: Speed
command matched no active samples" on the refactor branch) was this
race, and 2.1.0-rc.1 has it too: with messages under ~300 us apart most
speeds were dropped (docs/bugs.md has the measurements).

The command loop now keeps each play from its arrival until it starts
(src/pending_plays.rs, a pure library module). A command reaches such a
play the way it would reach the sound the play creates: by selector
(id, file or voice, OR'd as SampleSelector::matches does; internal_id
never matches, as it is assigned at start) or, for voice_stop and
voice_fade_out, by the voice the play names.

- speed, volume and seek take effect on the playing sounds at once and
  carry over onto the matching unstarted plays, which start with them.
  The last of each kind wins.
- stop, voice_stop and voice_fade_out cancel the matching unstarted
  plays, which are never heard. An HTTP client waiting on one gets 409,
  and the log says "Cancelled play of <file>: <reason>".
- A voice exists while a play naming it is loading (VoiceManager counts
  loading plays), so voice_volume reaches it without carrying, and a
  voice whose last sound stops keeps its volume while a play into it
  loads.

Nothing waits: commands never wait for loads and plays never wait for
each other. A command that reaches only unstarted plays reports
success; "not found" means it reached neither. A speed or seek that
handle_command ignores (a negative speed with pitch correction, or a
voice playing a stream) carries onto nothing; ignored_setting() now
decides that once for both paths, so the same warning is logged
wherever the command lands, and it is not reported as not found.

CommandScheduler drops the load generation counter. A play's load task
returns only its prepared audio and is matched back to its pending play
by task id. stopall and fadeall cancel every pending play and replace
the JoinSet, which aborts every load and drops any finished result. A
load task that panics now answers its play with 500 "Load of <file>
stopped unexpectedly" and logs the panic, where it got the 409 of a
cancelled load. A play whose HTTP client has gone is discarded when its
load ends, as before, and lets go of its voice.

docs/command-ordering.md has the rules and the differences from
one-at-a-time processing (stops cancel instead of interrupting; plays
start in the order their loads finish; a failed play held its voice
open while it loaded; windowed plays ignore a carried speed or seek
with a warning). commands.md, http-api.md, voice-management.md,
troubleshooting.md and architecture.md describe the behaviour, bugs.md
marks the ordering entry resolved with new measurements, and the
CHANGELOG has Changed and Fixed entries. The MQTT logging entry's
example (a speed for a sound that had not started) is no longer a
failure, so it now cites a sound that has ended.

Tests:
- pending_plays unit tests (16): matching by selector and voice,
  carrying, last-wins, cancelling, reaching without changing anything.
- VoiceManager unit tests (6): a loading play holds its voice open and
  keeps its volume; abandoning the last one ends the voice.
- tests/carry_over_property_test.rs: random command streams with random
  load completion orders and failures, checked against processing the
  same commands one at a time, a failed play counted as a sound until
  its load fails (2000 cases by default; 100 000 in release pass).
- 18 scheduler scenario tests in src/main.rs driving the real
  CommandScheduler with real WAVs, the real cache and an in-test HTTP
  server whose responses are held until released: 14 failed before the
  change, 3 guard behaviour that had to stay (a play never waits for an
  earlier one, internal_id never reaches an unstarted play, a play whose
  client left starts nothing), and 1 pins the ignored-speed reply, which
  failed against the first implementation.
- Characterization tests changed where behaviour changed on purpose:
  stopall's cancellation of an MQTT play is logged once at INFO as it
  happens, and a panicked load answers 500.

Reproduction: the MQTT sweep from docs/bugs.md against the release
build, now checking each sound's speed in /status/samples instead of
counting log lines: 180/180 sounds at 1.08x across warm gaps of 0 us to
5 ms and cold first plays at 0 to 20 ms, with no "Command failed" lines
(76 of the speeds were carried onto a loading play). voice_stop sent 0
to 1 ms after a looping play left 0 of 50 loops running (34 cancelled
before they started).

Validation: cargo fmt --check, cargo clippy --all-targets -D warnings,
and a warning-free release build (RUSTFLAGS=-D warnings); fuzz suite 7
passed; broker-backed native suite (MQTTAUDIO_BROKER_TESTS=1, TLS CA
set) 1022 passed, 0 failed, 7 device-gated ignored.

Prompts that led to this work:

- "I got this bug report from a customer who ran into a problem in the
  mqttaudio refactor branch related to sequencing/concurrency of message
  processing, then found it was fixed in the v2.1 branch. Can you
  investigate and determine if this is genuinely fixed, or if they just
  got lucky with a race condition?"
- "Let's go with the per-voice hold fix. Make sure to think through all
  the possible implications, like conflicting sequences of multiple
  commands affecting the same voice(s), or commands affecting multiple
  groups/voices/etc in a complex way, before designing our approach.
  We'll need a lot of tests in this area before implementation as well.
  Consider spawning adversarial review agents both before starting the
  fix and after implementing it."
- Decisions after the design review: "Carry-over (Recommended)" --
  commands never wait, and carry their settings onto plays that have
  not started; "Cancel it (Recommended)" -- stop, voice_stop and
  voice_fade_out cancel a matching play that has not started, with 409.

